WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.690
We learn about
conservation of energy in school.

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:03.980
How you can take potential energy,

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:06.500
like the energy in the
chemicals in this battery,

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:09.340
and you can then just convert
it to electrical energy.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:13.340
And then convert that to
whatever you can find a use for.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:16.270
A battery is just a store
of potential energy.

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:18.300
And that potential energy
could be chemical,

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.730
or it could be gravitational.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:25.230
This lake in this rainy bit of Wales
is part of the largest battery in Britain.

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:26.610
And it's rechargeable.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.770
Welcome to Dinorwig Power Station,

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:30.993
otherwise known as Electric Mountain.

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:35.320
- What we have is a large reservoir
at the top of the mountain,

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.040
a large reservoir at the bottom,

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.820
and between the two, a power station,

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:42.390
which converts that
potential energy into electricity.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:45.890
The power station itself is located
inside that mountain in deep caverns.

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:48.620
We release that potential energy
through the station.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:54.370
It drives a turbine that spins round at
synchronous speeds, connected to the grid.

00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:58.660
It drives a generator,
converting that into electricity.

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:01.670
We then export that
onto the National Grid.

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:05.100
And then at nighttime, we just
reverse the whole process.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.490
The turbine turns into a pump.
The generator becomes a motor.

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:13.150
And we push that water back up the hill
into the reservoir at Marchlyn Mawr,

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:14.240
at the top of the mountain.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.870
The operating principle
here is that the generation

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:19.210
that the station is able
to do during the daytime

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:22.170
helps the nation meet those peaks
of electricity demand.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:26.520
And overnight, it's able to pump the
water back when prices are lower.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.940
In electrical terms, we have
around nine gigawatt-hours

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.160
of storage at the top of the mountain.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:33.110
And in fact over the course of a day,

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:35.880
the water passing through the
station is broadly equivalent

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.650
to the total household
consumption of water in the UK.

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:40.530
At full load, we can
be meeting the demand

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:42.670
of around about two million homes.

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:44.610
- This sounds like a really simple idea,

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.830
but when there's this
much energy involved,

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:49.050
the design challenges are ridiculous.

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:51.350
If you've ever lived in a house
where you turn off a water tap

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:52.860
and the the pipes go clunk,

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.666
then you know about water hammer.

00:01:54.666 --> 00:01:57.480
Water isn't compressible, so closing a tap

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.130
means that all the water
that is under pressure

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:02.140
and rushing down the pipe
suddenly has nowhere to go.

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.020
So if the pipes in your house
aren't secured properly,

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.700
the crash and rebound of all that water

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:08.963
can make the plumbing move and make noise.

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:12.680
If that can happen with
just a tap in a house,

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.630
imagine what could happen
when one of the valves here closes.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:17.530
At full capacity, there
is somewhere around

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.750
a swimming pool every second

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:21.760
rushing through the turbines down there.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.870
When that valve closes, all
that water heading down here

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.790
suddenly has nowhere to go.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:29.210
The pressure would do
enormous amounts of damage.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:33.290
It would break everything apart,
if it wasn't for one extra bit of plumbing.

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:37.110
A shaft hundreds of metres deep
that acts as a pressure release.

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:39.270
We can't see anything, on the
surface or from down here, sadly,

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:41.560
it all happens
deep within the mountain.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.330
But every time those valves move,

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:45.890
[valve clanking]

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:48.400
that surge pipe is what
stops the whole system

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.350
from tearing itself apart.

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:53.450
This station is not some
quirky old curiosity.

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:55.170
This is serious engineering

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:57.980
that keeps the lights
on around the country.

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:00.290
- Dinorwig Power Station
is one of the largest

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:02.440
power stations of its type in the world,

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:05.740
and it's also one of the
fastest-acting power stations.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.570
So as well as being able to take advantage

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:10.300
of the peaks and troughs
in the electricity market,

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:12.170
it was essentially built, primarily,

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:15.820
with the intent to provide very fast response onto the system

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:20.840
in order to help the National Grid balance
supply and demand instantaneously

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:22.760
on a second-by-second basis.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:26.960
So it has the capacity to be able to go
from zero up to its full capacity,

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:30.310
maybe 2,000 megawatts,
within 10 to 30 seconds.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:32.100
When people talk about pumped storage,

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.820
they tend to associate
it a lot with TV pickups,

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:37.860
and National Grid, in analysing that risk,

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:42.250
study the TV schedules and plan ahead
and put Dinorwig on standby, if you like,

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:44.460
to be able to meet those pickups.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:47.070
Going back to the 80s and the 90s,

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:49.710
everyone would be sitting
around the same TV channels

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:51.170
with a limited choice, then.

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:55.720
All the ingredients you need for a sort-of
collective putting-on of the kettle

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:58.400
and opening of the fridge.
Pickups could be pretty significant.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:01.840
But since then, people are streaming
on YouTube, on Netflix,

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:05.520
all the different plethora of channels
that we have, so that has changed

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.490
that sort of coincidence
of viewing on one channel.

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:10.869
The current environment that we're in,

00:04:10.869 --> 00:04:15.540
we have significant amount of renewable
technology on the system, which provides

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:18.450
a level of unpredictability day to day.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:20.740
And actually, the
importance of storage now

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:26.410
is that we can use it to help develop
the amount of renewables we have

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:28.420
and allow us to fill in those gaps

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:31.520
when the wind's not blowing
and the sun is not shining.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.660
So having storage is such an important
component of the modern grid system.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:37.540
- Thank you very much
to all the team at Engie

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:38.650
who made this visit possible.

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:40.430
Pull down the description
for more about them,

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:42.163
and more about Electric Mountain.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:55.737
A battery is just... a store!...
of potential energy.

